    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 311946\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1246 PM PDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* A few showers and thunderstorms remain possible through early\n  this evening with localized heavy downpours, mainly across\n  southern Nevada.\n\n* Widespread wetting rain moves in late tonight and continues\n  through Sunday afternoon. Locally heavy rainfall and a few\n  instances of flash flooding will be possible. \n\n* Conditions dry out on Monday as gusty north winds pick up across\n  the region. Temperatures remain slightly below normal next week.\n\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through next weekend.\n\nAfternoon satellite imagery depicts a broad cloud shield extending\nnortheastward off our approaching low pressure system off the \nPacific Coast into the southern Great Basin. Meanwhile, to the \nsouth of this feature, filtered sunshine and modest clearing are \nallowing for some minor instability to develop across southern \nNevada and northwest Arizona. With a little additional heating, \nsurface based CAPE values are forecast to climb into the 250-500 \nj/kg range, will will be just enough to spark a few isolated \nshowers and storms again this afternoon. Storms today are not \nexpected to be as intense or widespread as observed Friday thanks \nto a sharp warming aloft and weaker wind shear, but the \nenvironment remains quite moist so brief but locally heavy \ndownpours will remain possible. \n\nBy tonight, precipitation coverage will shift from predominantly\nconvective to almost entirely stratiform as considerable \nsubtropical moisture overspreads the region. This is in response \nto an impressive low pressure system off the SoCal coast that will\nmove inland through Sunday. Todays forecast guidance appears to\nhave converged on a common theme in terms of precipitation totals\nand coverage areas - with increased confidence in locally heavy\nrainfall totals exceeding 1 inch across Lincoln and northeastern\nClark Counties. In fact, this mornings guidance suggests 24 hour\nprecipitation totals in the Mesquite area may rival or exceed 24 \nhour totals rarely seen in a typical ten year interval. There is\nalso concern that additional rainfall will cause additional\nflooding in the Colorado River Valley which is still recovering\nfrom pockets of locally heavy flooding on Friday. With these\nconsiderations in mind, went ahead and expanded the Flash Flood\nWatch further west. The flood watch remains in place for Northwest\nArizona as well where confidence remains high that heavy rainfall\nwill occur.\n\nCloser to the Las Vegas Valley, some degree of rain shadowing\nremains expected which will work to limit the intensity of the\nrainfall. Nonetheless, still anticipate several periods of\nrain starting by late tonight and continuing into at least Sunday\nmorning. Rainfall totals in general are expected to range in the \n0.25-0.50 range, though there are hints the far southwest Vegas \nValley may be a little lower due to the rainshadow effect. Periods\nof heavier rainfall are expected in the Spring Mountains, with \nrainfall totals exceeding 1 inch very likely. Snow levels will \nremain above 9000 feet through the event so we're not anticipating\nany snow related impacts beyond accumulations at the top of \nCharleston Peak and higher reaches of Lee Canyon ski area. Points \nfurther west across Inyo county and western San Bernardino County \ncan generally expect more showery conditions with generally light \nrainfall totals expected.\n\nConditions will tend to dry out from west to east Sunday\nafternoon, though pockets of embedded thunderstorms are expected \n- especially across Northwest Arizona where flood risk will remain\nelevated. By Sunday night, drier conditions will overspread the \nregion with northwesterly breezes filtering in behind the storm \nsystem. \n\nThe remainder of the week looks quiet through there may be some\npatchy morning fog in low lying and moist valleys which observed\nsignificant rainfall in the past week. Temperatures will hover a\nfew degrees below normal. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Sky \nconditions gradually deteriorate throughout the day as ceilings \nlower and precipitation chances increase. Isolated showers (and a \n10% chance of thunder) are possible as early as mid-afternoon, but \nthe more widespread precipitation arrives tonight. Latest guidance \nhas a 50% chance of CIGs getting down to 2kft between 09z and 13z \nSunday. Some visibility reductions due to rain are possible, but \nprecipitation intensity should mostly be on the light side. Some \nimprovement in sky conditions is anticipated Sunday afternoon, but \ntiming and extent are still uncertain. South-southwest winds will be \non the increase as this occurs.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Moisture and cloud cover \ncontinue to increase, with the best precipitation chances along and \nnorth of I-15 through the afternoon. This evening and overnight, \nrain chances spread to areas south and east. Precipitation intensity \nwill largely be on the lighter side, but periods of increased rain \nrates and reduced visibility are possible. Expecting CIGs to drop \ndown to 2-4kft across most of the area tonight and Sunday morning, \nresulting in MVFR conditions and significant terrain obscuration. \nGusty south winds expected across our northwestern zones today, with \nlighter breezes elsewhere.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Outler\nAVIATION...Woods\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
